            <div xml:id="d214">

                <pb facs="0282" n="212" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv01/pg212"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.731/118: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Special Representative</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >House</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-11-16T22:00:00+00:00"
                            >November 16, 1918—10 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>114. Secret for the President. I suggest that you send me a cable which I can
                    show to the heads of British and French Governments for the purpose of obtaining
                    from them the entire suspension of the present political censorship upon
                    American press despatches; military necessity can no longer be invoked as a
                    defense of the drastic censorship now being exercised. There seems to be in my
                    opinion no adequate reason why the character of the political information
                    supplied to the American people should be dictated by the French and British
                    Governments.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward House</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
